{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-2751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global maximum value of the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ on the closed interval $4\\le x\\le 9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-2751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x>0.$ So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=4,9$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ f(9) & =\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(9)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The least value is $f(4)=2,$ so the global minimum is $2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/c8badccb8144447b5b57157308b5614293a54569f8247320a358dd20650d91a1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-2751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c58a01c4f0e9a0216b6276ba4d5fb66deda16f750ba7ae88cf7fd11a916192b4"} {"question" "q-2751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-2751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-2751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-22568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x},$ defined on the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},2],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}$\n- The global minimum value is $2\\sqrt{e}$\n- The global minimum value is $4\\sqrt[4]{e}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-22568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot x-{e}^{x}\\cdot 1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(x-1)}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}(x-1)}{{x}^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=1,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{{e}^{1/2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2\\sqrt{e}≈3.3 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{1}=e≈2.7 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}≈3.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(2)=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=e,$ so the global minimum is $e.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/98156f05b9b6c71c7e5a5753d024d0851a0a779741545ba2d62d3d63af41e824.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-22568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c58a01c4f0e9a0216b6276ba4d5fb66deda16f750ba7ae88cf7fd11a916192b4"} {"question" "q-22568" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-22568" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-22568" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1$ on the closed interval $[-1,3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3{x}^{2}+6x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+6x & =0 \\\\ -3x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,2,$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}-1=3 \\\\ f(0) & =-(0)^{3}+3(0)^{2}-1=-1 \\\\ f(2) & =-(2)^{3}+3(2)^{2}-1=3 \\\\ f(3) & =-(3)^{3}+3(3)^{2}-1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=f(2)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=f(3)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/ebc8acabf87ca5c3423f2f7c07637a87c5963d9cb080a4d52f9efd87846380d1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c58a01c4f0e9a0216b6276ba4d5fb66deda16f750ba7ae88cf7fd11a916192b4"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-79342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ defined on the closed interval $[-2,1],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global minimum value is $-1$\n- The global maximum value is $1$\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1({x}^{2}+1)-x(2x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\pm 1,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(-2)^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ f(-1) & =-\\frac{(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+1}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=-\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global minimum is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/f3165b8b46c8ac0ab798ae6162d4b4facb92367f5072598bef936801bce44fc4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c58a01c4f0e9a0216b6276ba4d5fb66deda16f750ba7ae88cf7fd11a916192b4"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-79348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-344373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The global maximum value of the function $f(x)=-x+4$ on the closed interval $1\\le x\\le 5$ is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-344373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-x+4,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all real values of $x.$ So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=1,5$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-(1)+4=3 \\\\ f(5) & =-(5)+4=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(5)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/b66a275ad0f733f9b7a715cacc87e47ab42200def67a3a7f5a5e577ba067a1c3.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-344373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c58a01c4f0e9a0216b6276ba4d5fb66deda16f750ba7ae88cf7fd11a916192b4"} {"question" "q-344373" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-344373" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-314729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The exact global minimum value of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1$ is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-314729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1],$ the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)^{4}-4(-1)^{3}+1=2+4+1=7 \\\\ f(0) & =2(0)^{4}-4(0)^{3}+1=1 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)^{4}-4(1)^{3}+1=2-4+1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=7,$ so the global maximum is $7.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/fa4a3f723bb55521ec02dda8d088427b1939721366155f5ac45dd8e1429992e7.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-314729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c58a01c4f0e9a0216b6276ba4d5fb66deda16f750ba7ae88cf7fd11a916192b4"} {"question" "q-314729" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-314729" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-2690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x+3,$ on the closed interval $-4\\le x\\le -2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $3$\n- The global minimum value is $1$\n- The global minimum value is $-1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-2690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+12x+9 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+4x+3) \\\\  & =3(x+1)(x+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n3(x+1)(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\\,-1.\n$$\n\nSince $x=-1$ does not belong to the interval $[-4,-2]$, the critical points are $x=-3$ and the endpoints $x=-4,-2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =(-4)^{3}+6(-4)^{2}+9(-4)+3=-1 \\\\ f(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+6(-3)^{2}+9(-3)+3=3 \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2)^{3}+6(-2)^{2}+9(-2)+3=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-3)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The least value is $f(-4)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/fa85119166ca325f9925ddd03a578fb5daae463c59c3b7b9f7ac0f6464a30241.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-2690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c58a01c4f0e9a0216b6276ba4d5fb66deda16f750ba7ae88cf7fd11a916192b4"} {"question" "q-2690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-2690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-2690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-344443" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The global minimum value of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le 2\\pi $ is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-344443" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ since they are the only values that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ and the endpoints $x=0,2\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0 \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1 \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ f(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1,$ so the global maximum is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/c5368ebe232e9ad044cacb16300fb2f3f1747fefd326f4be4e0c22d0e3a7069d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-344443" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c58a01c4f0e9a0216b6276ba4d5fb66deda16f750ba7ae88cf7fd11a916192b4"} {"question" "q-344443" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-344443" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-20979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=13-9{x}^{2}+2{x}^{3},$ on the interval $[-1,2],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $2$\n- The global maximum value is $13$\n- The global minimum value is $-7$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-20979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=13-9{x}^{2}+2{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-18x+6{x}^{2} \\\\  & =6x(x-3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n6x(x-3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\,3.\n$$\n\nSince $x=3$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,2]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =13-9(-1)^{2}+2(-1)^{3}=2 \\\\ f(0) & =13-9(0)^{2}+2(0)^{3}=13 \\\\ f(2) & =13-9(2)^{2}+2(2)^{3}=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=13,$ so the global maximum is $13.$\n\n- The least value is $f(2)=-7,$ so the global minimum is $-7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/1a9d2f0db241c0a209c315a8e901e40a44258f339bbaa7bee36198b58fe2684d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-20979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c58a01c4f0e9a0216b6276ba4d5fb66deda16f750ba7ae88cf7fd11a916192b4"} {"question" "q-20979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-20979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-20979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-2721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find  the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=3{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+3$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-2721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=3{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =3(-1)^{4}-8(-1)^{3}+3=14 \\\\ f(0) & =3(0)^{4}-8(0)^{3}+3=3 \\\\ f(1) & =3(1)^{4}-8(1)^{3}+3=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=14,$ so the global maximum is $14.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=-2,$ so the global minimum is $-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/9ac7f0c0e28532c897e1c89a35e9c027f614fee40b4b94362a6c5cb012c8a7f6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-2721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c58a01c4f0e9a0216b6276ba4d5fb66deda16f750ba7ae88cf7fd11a916192b4"} {"question" "q-2721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-2721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "q-2721" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/state.json" "file_sha256" "20227ab3367ea74656ecab539753df7a2beffa323e20f369ae8d87474a5a00c7"} {"question" "e-3736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global extrema of $f(x) = 2x - x^2$  on the interval $[-1,4].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/example-3736.json" "file_sha256" "45c5e175cafa8665f153bf96d8285242e4662cf29b0c58ce20ac7fe572dc485b"} {"question" "e-3736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2x-x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 2-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n2-2x = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1.\n$$\n\nThe critical points are $x=1$ and the endpoints $x=-1,4$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)-(-1)^{2}=-3 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)-(1)^{2}=1 \\\\ f(4) & =2(4)-(4)^{2}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=1,$ so the global maximum value is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(4)=-8,$ so the global minimum value is $-8.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/c277beb3423cc80d3ac2a001ac1063059b645c65fc8cb77978050f3831274b31.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/example-3736.json" "file_sha256" "45c5e175cafa8665f153bf96d8285242e4662cf29b0c58ce20ac7fe572dc485b"} {"question" "e-3737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global maximum and minimum values for the function $f(x) = x^4 - 6 x^2$ on the closed interval $[-1, 3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/example-3737.json" "file_sha256" "955df653b9e0475549b7b74de471662e7b66593e9c8626032c24c93fd024d614"} {"question" "e-3737" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x^4 - 6x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 12x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-12x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ 4x(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x = -\\sqrt 3 \\approx -1.73$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, we ignore this point.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,\\sqrt{3}$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(-1) \t& = (-1)^4 - 6(-1)^2 = -5\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(0) \t\t& = (0)^4 - 6(0)^2 = 0\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(\\sqrt 3) \t& = (\\sqrt 3)^4 - 6(\\sqrt 3)^2 = -9\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(3) \t\t& = (3)^4 - 6(3)^2 = 27 \\\t\t\n \t\t}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the global maximum is $27$ and the global minimum is $-9.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/74ce5114dbc93035b0be078be12149cced66ce881dfbc37768ed9febcdfcba87.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/example-3737.json" "file_sha256" "955df653b9e0475549b7b74de471662e7b66593e9c8626032c24c93fd024d614"}] "usage" {"e-3737" [] "e-3736" []} "basis" 1554 "retractions" [[17592186063962 :question/problem "Find the global extrema of $f(x) = 2x - x^2$ on the interval $[-1,4]$."] [17592186063962 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 2-2x\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0$, we get\n\n$$\n2-2x = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1\n$$\n\nThe critical points are $x=1$ and the endpoints $x=-1,4$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-1) &= 2(-1) - (-1)^{2} =-3 \\\\\nf(1) &= 2(1) - (1)^{2} = 1 \\\\\nf(4) &= 2(4) - (4)^{2} =-8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2$.\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=1$, so the global maximum value is $1$.\n- The least value is $f(4)=-8$, so the global minimum value is $-8$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/364/Source/Images/a3da8fc5afb2bade8dcf669299f130d8.png)"] [17592186063963 :question/problem "Find the global maximum and minimum values for the function $f(x) = x^4 - 6 x^2$ on the closed interval $[-1, 3]$."] [17592186063963 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the critical points. Taking the derivative of\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^4 - 6x^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 12x\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{3} - 12x &= 0 \\\\\n4x(x^{2} - 3) &= 0 \\\\\n4x(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - \\sqrt{3}) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nx = -\\sqrt 3 \\approx -1.73\n$$\n\ndoes not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, we ignore this point.\n\nSo, the critical points are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nand the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(-1) & = (-1)^4 - 6(-1)^2 = -5 \\\\[5pt] f(0) & = (0)^4 - 6(0)^2 = 0 \\\\[5pt] f(\\sqrt 3) & = (\\sqrt 3)^4 - 6(\\sqrt 3)^2 = -9 \\\\[5pt] f(3) & = (3)^4 - 6(3)^2 = 27 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the global maximum is $27$ and the global minimum is $-9$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/364/Source/Images/80f32cf65aaa0b3f582aff95ebb1352f.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
